  ],
  "details": "<p>This\u00a0<span class=\"highlightedSearchTerm\">archive</span>\u00a0is located in the\u00a0Nab\u00fb Temple.</p>\r\n<p>\u00a0<img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"356\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan66_general_plan_Kalhu1-17_p144.png\" width=\"385\" /></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"344\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan70_Nabu_Temple_Kalhu14-16_p153.png\" width=\"369\" /></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "British excavators found fragments of at least eight or nine large clay tablets in the Throne Room of the Nab\u00fb Temple (Ezida). These now-fragmentarily-preserved tablets, all of which date to 672 BC, contain copies of treaties that seventh-century-BC Assyrian king Esarhaddon (r. 680\u2013669 BC) made with several Median client rulers. These \u2018covenant\u2019 documents \u2014 which are often referred to as \u201cEsarhaddon\u2019s Succession Treaty\u201d or \u201cEsarhaddon\u2019s Vassal Treaty\u201d \u2014 were written up to ensure loyalty to Ashurbanipal, Esarhaddon\u2019s fourth eldest son, when he ascended the Assyrian throne upon the death of his father.",
  "reprPoint": [
